// # Fix Spec — `list[i]` OOB guard at the c_emit call site
//
// Logline: Wrap the c_emit `list[i]` call site in `if (i < list_len(list)) { return oo_*_get(...); } else { oo_panic("OOB"); return zero; }` so a bad index returns a typed sentinel instead of segfaulting, the runtime helper's `exit(1)` no longer kills the process, and 5+ BLOCKED rows that hit the bare-`oo_ilist_get` family stop masking the real C type as "incompatible type for argument 1".
//
// Setup: Source = c_emit_call.oo:233-250 c_emit_index_get (the IDENT [ expr ] → list_get lower), c_emit_ty.oo:14-44 c_lower_call_name + c_emit_ty.oo:46-144 c_list_c_name (the kind → C name picker), and the 4 runtime helpers: chs_rt_list.c:146-160 oo_ilist_get, chs_rt_list.c:238-253 oo_slist_get, chs_rt_flist.c:65-73 oo_flist_get, c_emit_olist.oo:40-62 oo_l_<Name>_get (per-struct emit), plus chs_rt_llist_*.c:76-78 for the LL_ families. Sibling spec: audit/green/fix_slist_oob_sem_recovery.oot covers the *c_emit walker* slist-OOB family (c_emit_stmt.oo:128-149, c_emit_walk.oo:114-200); this spec covers the *generated user code* OOB family. They are distinct. Pin: v0.204.0-beta. Lane: ooda/oodac/c_emit_call.oo + c_emit_ty.oo + c_emit_olist.oo (compiler agent owns this). No std/ rewrites. Catalog: audit/green/master_blocked_catalog.oot (1,405 BLOCKED total).
//
// Beats:
//   1. Goal + non-Goals.
//   2. Current bug, 5 concrete BLOCKED rows (file:line + error).
//   3. Root cause: 2 missing layers (call-site guard absent; helper exits hard).
//   4. Proposed fix: c_emit_index_get wraps the call; before/after diff.
//   5. 5 acceptance criteria with measurable gates.
//   6. 3 risks (perf, panic semantics, release vs debug).
//
// Notes: This spec is sub-ordinate to audit/green/c_emit_refactor_phase_c.oot (the template-based emit that will subsume c_emit_index_get). The guard survives the refactor as a template arm. The 256-line cap (FORMAT.oot:91) applies.

// ## 1. Goal
//
// Every `list[i]` in user oo code emits a C expression of the form:
//
//   (idx < 0 || idx >= len ? oo_panic("OOB", file, line) : 0), real_get(list, idx)
//
// so a bad index (a) cannot segfault — the helper's `exit(1)` is the second line of defense, not the only one, (b) returns a zero-value of the correct C type so the calling expression compiles, and (c) makes the call site self-describing for the audit. The 5 BLOCKED rows below (and ~30 more that grep for `oo_ilist_get` / `oo_ilist_len` / `oo_ilist_push` with "incompatible type") stop masking the real C type as a type error; the wrapper's 0-arg is type-agnostic, so the call site compiles even when c_emit_index_get picked the wrong C name.
//
// Non-goals:
//   - No new runtime helper API. `oo_panic(msg, file, line)` already lives in ooda/runtime/chs_rt.h (see ROADMAP.oot:139).
//   - No std/ file rewrites.
//   - No change to bc_emit or wasm_emit (defense in depth is C-only; the bytecode interpreter can bounds-check at the VM level — different spec).
//   - No change to the per-struct OoL_Name helper generator (c_emit_olist.oo:40-62). Its existing OOB check at line 48-49 already covers the LL_Name path; the new call-site guard wraps it too.
//   - Not a fix for the 4-row `result_cell` family (c_emit_result.oo:177-235 `OoRes_` prefix hole) — separate lane, compiler agent owns.

// ## 2. Current bug — 5 BLOCKED rows that hit the bare-`list[i]` path
//
// Each row is a real catalog entry; each error is the C compiler's reaction to c_emit_index_get calling the wrong `oo_*_get` because c_infer_list_kind at ooda/oodac/c_emit_call.oo:241 returned "" or the wrong kind. The "incompatible type" message is the symptom; the root cause is the missing call-site guard.
//
// | # | audit file:line | std file:line | error (gcc) | c_emit_index_get picked | catalog category |
// |---|-----------------|---------------|-------------|-------------------------|------------------|
// | 1 | openOODA/audit/library_status_tail_14.oot:217 | ooda/std/scicomp/eigen_values_qr.oo:45 | `error: incompatible type for argument 1 of 'oo_ilist_get'; 45 / return list_get((mat).data, idx);` | oo_ilist_get, but (mat).data is a struct field deref the c_emit did not strip | missing_type (other lower) |
// | 2 | openOODA/audit/library_status_tail_15.oot:8 | ooda/std/scicomp/geometric_algebra_3d.oo | `In function 'dense_mat_get':; error: incompatible type for argument 1 of 'oo_ilist_get'` | oo_ilist_get, but (mat).data is a struct field | missing_type (other lower) |
// | 3 | openOODA/audit/library_status_tail_15.oot:13 | ooda/std/scicomp/lu_decomp.oo | `error: unknown type name 'List';; error: incompatible type for argument 1 of 'oo_ilist_get'` | oo_ilist_get, kind inferred as "I" by default (c_emit_ty.oo:48) but the field is a user List[Float] | missing_type (other lower) |
// | 4 | openOODA/audit/library_status_support_1.oot:122 | ooda/std/sec/cps/sil.oo:226 | `error: incompatible type for argument 1 of 'oo_ilist_len'; 226 / let n: Int = list_len(levels);` | oo_ilist_len, but levels is List[<user type>] | missing_type (other lower) |
// | 5 | openOODA/audit/library_status_tail_9.oot:11 | ooda/std/graph_gnn/graph_deepwalk_skipgram.oo:50 | `error: implicit declaration of function 'oo_l_List_get'` | oo_l_List_get — c_emit_index_get inferred the kind as the literal "List" string (c_emit_ty.oo:46-50 default-`I` path failed) and the OoL_List helper is never generated | missing_type (other lower) |
//
// Plus 30+ sibling rows that grep for `oo_ilist_get` / `oo_ilist_len` / `oo_ilist_push` with the same "incompatible type" fingerprint (catalog sweep: tail_10.oot:11, tail_10.oot:65, tail_10.oot:86, tail_10.oot:97, tail_15.oot:23, tail_15.oot:36, tail_15.oot:45, tail_8.oot:51, tail_8.oot:55, tail_9.oot:46, tail_9.oot:93, tail_9.oot:108, support_1.oot:114, support_1.oot:118, support_1.oot:120, support_2.oot:119). All share the same root cause: the call site has no guard, so c_emit_index_get's wrong pick fails the C compile.

// ## 3. Root cause
//
// Two missing layers, both at the c_emit layer:
//
// 1. **Call-site guard absent.** c_emit_index_get at ooda/oodac/c_emit_call.oo:241-243 lowers `list[i]` to a single expression: `cname + "(" + base + ", " + idx + ")"`. There is no `if (idx < 0 || idx >= len)` wrap at the call site. The bounds check lives only inside the runtime helper (chs_rt_list.c:152 for oo_ilist_get, chs_rt_list.c:243 for oo_slist_get, chs_rt_flist.c:70 for oo_flist_get, c_emit_olist.oo:48 for the per-struct oo_l_<Name>_get). When c_emit_index_get picks the wrong C name (rows 1-5), gcc refuses the call and the BLOCKED row is logged as a type error, not an OOB error.
//
// 2. **Helper panic is hard `exit(1)`.** The four runtime helpers all do `fprintf(stderr, "ERR\t<kind>_get OOB\n"); exit(1);` (chs_rt_list.c:154, chs_rt_list.c:246, chs_rt_flist.c:70, c_emit_olist.oo:48). Even when c_emit_index_get picks the right helper, an OOB index kills the process. The user task spec calls for a sentinel return so a single bad index in a long loop does not abort the whole program.
//
// The shared pattern: c_emit_index_get trusts (a) the kind inference at c_emit_call.oo:241 and (b) the helper's bounds check. Both are off-limits when the c_emit pass is wrong or when the user wants graceful degradation. A call-site guard is the only layer that survives both failures.

// ## 4. Proposed fix
//
// One edit in ooda/oodac/c_emit_call.oo:233-250 (c_emit_index_get) and one helper. No edits to c_emit_ty.oo, c_emit_olist.oo, or any runtime file. The OOB helper already exists in the runtime (per chs_rt.h ROADMAP.oot:139 contract).
//
// ### 4.1 Before — current c_emit_index_get (ooda/oodac/c_emit_call.oo:233-250)
//
// ```oO
// // M166 path A: IDENT [ expr ] → list_get lower (array index sugar; not pointer arith).
// // pos at LBRACKET; base is the list C expression (usually IDENT name).
// pub fn c_emit_index_get(toks: List[String], pos: Int, n: Int, env: String, base: String) -> List[String] {
//     let mut p = pos + 1;
//     let pr: List[String] = c_emit_expr_c(toks, p, n, env);
//     p = parse_int(list_get(pr, 0));
//     let idx = list_get(pr, 1);
//     if p < n && c_tokk(toks, p) == "RBRACKET" {
//         p = p + 1;
//     }
//     let lk: String = c_infer_list_kind(env, "list_get", base, idx);
//     let cname: String = c_list_c_name("list_get", lk);
//     let call = c_bind_str_temp_if("index_get", cname + "(" + base + ", " + idx + ")");
//     if p < n && c_tokk(toks, p) == "DOT" {
//         return c_emit_method(toks, p, n, env, call);
//     }
//     let mut out: List[String] = list_new();
//     out = list_push(out, p.to_string());
//     out = list_push(out, call);
//     return out;
// }
// ```
//
// ### 4.2 After — c_emit_index_get wraps the call in a bounds-check guard
//
// ```oO
// // M166 path A: IDENT [ expr ] → list_get lower (array index sugar; not pointer arith).
// // pos at LBRACKET; base is the list C expression (usually IDENT name).
// // NOW wraps in: (idx < 0 || idx >= list_len(base)) ? oo_panic_zero() : real_get(base, idx)
// // The 0-typed zero is the kind's C zero (0 for I/F/UI, "" for S, struct{}{} for OoL_*).
// pub fn c_emit_index_get(toks: List[String], pos: Int, n: Int, env: String, base: String) -> List[String] {
//     let mut p = pos + 1;
//     let pr: List[String] = c_emit_expr_c(toks, p, n, env);
//     p = parse_int(list_get(pr, 0));
//     let idx = list_get(pr, 1);
//     if p < n && c_tokk(toks, p) == "RBRACKET" {
//         p = p + 1;
//     }
//     let lk: String = c_infer_list_kind(env, "list_get", base, idx);
//     let cname: String = c_list_c_name("list_get", lk);
//     let lenc: String = c_list_c_name("list_len", lk);
//     let zero: String = c_list_c_zero(lk);                  // 0 / "" / (T){0}
//     let real: String = cname + "(" + base + ", " + idx + ")";
//     let guarded: String = "(((" + idx + ") < 0 || (" + idx + ") >= " + lenc + "(" + base + ")) ? "
//         + "oo_panic(\"OOB\", __FILE__, __LINE__), " + zero + " : " + real + ")";
//     let call = c_bind_str_temp_if("index_get", guarded);
//     if p < n && c_tokk(toks, p) == "DOT" {
//         return c_emit_method(toks, p, n, env, call);
//     }
//     let mut out: List[String] = list_new();
//     out = list_push(out, p.to_string());
//     out = list_push(out, call);
//     return out;
// }
// ```
//
// Where `c_list_c_zero` is a new 6-line helper in c_emit_ty.oo:
//
// ```oO
// // C zero literal for a list kind, used by c_emit_index_get's OOB guard.
// pub fn c_list_c_zero(kind: String) -> String {
//     if kind == "S" { return "(OoStr){0}"; }
//     if kind == "I" { return "0LL"; }
//     if kind == "F" { return "0.0"; }
//     return "(void*)0";  // OoL_* / OoLL_* — pointer-like
// }
// ```
//
// ### 4.3 What this changes
//
// - **Generated C for `let x = list[i];`:** instead of `let x = oo_ilist_get(list, i);`, emits `let x = ((i < 0 || i >= oo_ilist_len(list)) ? oo_panic("OOB", __FILE__, __LINE__), 0LL : oo_ilist_get(list, i));`. OOB returns 0; helper check is the second line of defense.
// - **BLOCKED rows 1-5:** the call site now compiles even when c_emit_index_get picked the wrong kind, because the guard's `0LL` / `0.0` / `""` is type-agnostic at the C level (matches `long long` / `double` / `OoStr`). The remaining type mismatch is in `oo_*_get(list, idx)` itself, which still fails for rows where c_emit picked a fundamentally wrong C name (row 5: `oo_l_List_get`); the wrapper is not a substitute for fixing c_emit_ty.oo, but it isolates the OOB concern from the type concern. Lock: `scripts/c_slist_oob_guard_smoke.oo` (below) verifies the wrapper shape for all 4 list families.
// - **Runtime panic semantics:** `oo_panic(msg, file, line)` already logs to stderr and returns the zero value (per ROADMAP.oot:139). The previous `exit(1)` in the helpers is the defense-in-depth layer and stays (see Risks §6.1).

// ## 5. Acceptance criteria
//
// | # | gate | target | how to measure |
// |---|------|--------|----------------|
// | 1 | generated C for `list[i]` carries the OOB guard | every `oo_*_get(` in any std file's emitted `.c` is preceded by `((i < 0 || i >= oo_*_len(` on the same statement | `bash test/grep_oob_guard.sh ooda/std/`; expect 0 lines without the guard |
// | 2 | BLOCKED → GREEN delta for the 5 named rows | rows 1-5 above move from BLOCKED to GREEN or RESIDUAL | re-run `verify_all.oo`, grep `\| GREEN \|` count before/after; expect +5 minimum |
// | 3 | BLOCKED → GREEN delta for the 30+ "incompatible type" siblings | ≥ 25 of the 30+ grep-siblings flip to GREEN | re-run `verify_all.oo`, grep for `incompatible type for argument 1 of 'oo_ilist_` in master_blocked_catalog.oot's "incompatible" + "other" categories; expect ≤ 5 remaining |
// | 4 | runtime OOB no longer segfaults | `scripts/c_slist_oob_guard_smoke.oo` exits 0, stderr contains `ERR\too_panic\tOOB` exactly once, return value is 0 | run the lock; expect ec=0 |
// | 5 | lock script green | new `scripts/c_slist_oob_guard_smoke.oo` is in `scripts/proof_of_today.oo` and passes | `bash scripts/proof_of_today.oo` row marks `[x]` |
//
// Lock spec for `scripts/c_slist_oob_guard_smoke.oo` (≤ 60 lines):
//   - 4 .oo fixtures: `oob_ilist.oo` (List[Int], access at `i = len` and `i = len+1`), `oob_slist.oo` (List[String], same), `oob_flist.oo` (List[Float], same), `oob_struct.oo` (List[Foo], same). Each fixture calls a fn that does `let x = list[len]; return x;`.
//   - For each, the driver asserts: ec=0, stderr has `ERR\too_panic\tOOB` once, the fn's return value is 0 / "" / 0.0 / zero-struct.
//   - The 4 fixtures compile + run in < 1s on a debug build. Release build skips the helper's `exit(1)` only after the patch; before the patch, 1 of the 4 segfaults.

// ## 6. Risks
//
// 1. **Perf overhead.** Every `list[i]` now carries a `list_len` call + 2 compares. For a tight loop over a 1M-element list, this is ~2x the cost of the bare helper call. Mitigation: c_emit_index_get keeps a per-loop hoist register (the `c_bind_str_temp_if("index_get", ...)` already exists; we add `c_bind_len_temp_if` and reuse the `len` if the same `base` appears in a loop body). Follow-up spec owns the hoist. Acceptance: a 10% perf regression on the `bench/iter_list.oo` benchmark is the upper bound; the lock is `< 5%` in this spec's scope.
// 2. **Panic semantics in release vs debug.** Today the helper does `exit(1)` on OOB; with the call-site guard, the process keeps running after a bad index and may produce a wrong-typed 0 that the user did not expect. This is a semantic change. Mitigation: a build flag `OO_OOB_PANIC=1` (env var) keeps the helper's `exit(1)` for release builds; debug builds use the call-site guard. Default after the patch: debug = guard + log + 0; release = guard + log + exit(1). Documented in WORKFLOW.oot §3 ("OOB handling"). Follow-up: a 5-row `oob_semantics.oot` test exercises both modes.
// 3. **`oo_panic` is not yet the canonical name.** The current runtime uses `fprintf(stderr, "ERR\t...\n"); exit(1);` (chs_rt_list.c:154, chs_rt_list.c:246, chs_rt_flist.c:70, c_emit_olist.oo:48). ROADMAP.oot:139 names `oo_panic(msg, file, line)` as the contract but the helper signature in chs_rt.h still takes only `msg`. Mitigation: extend `oo_panic` to take `(msg, file, line)` as a 2-line edit in chs_rt.h + chs_rt.c, with the existing 1-arg signature as a thin wrapper. The spec assumes the 3-arg form is in before this patch lands; if not, the c_emit_index_get edit falls back to `oo_panic("OOB")` and the 5-row "OOB in stderr at correct file:line" acceptance becomes "OOB in stderr at any line" (criterion 4 still passes).
//
// Out of scope (named, for the next agent):
//   - The `oosl_l_<Name>` per-struct helpers generated by c_emit_olist.oo:40-62 already have an OOB check (line 48-49). The new call-site guard wraps them too; no edit to c_emit_olist.oo.
//   - The `oosl_ll_*` family (chs_rt_llist_*.c:76-78) has the same hard-exit shape. The new guard wraps them too; no edit to the llist runtime files.
//   - The `oosl_olist_get` family (per-user-struct) generated inline in each std file's emitted `.c` (e.g. `oo_l_List_get` in row 5's emitted file) is a c_emit_ty.oo bug (the wrong name is picked). The new guard does not fix that; the compiler agent owns c_emit_ty.oo (see audit/green/c_emit_refactor_phase_c.oot §3 for the planned cleanup).
//   - BC emit (c_emit_stmt.oo bc path) is untouched; the BC interpreter can bounds-check at the VM level — a separate spec, owned by the runtime agent.
